I added `CurrentPageBadge` at `app/javascript/mastodon/components/current_page_badge.tsx`. I could not fully verify it: the repo has no `node_modules`, so ESLint won't run and `tsc` fails on missing type packages before it reaches this file. The file is unverified beyond reading it.

```tsx
import { FormattedMessage } from 'react-intl';

import { Badge } from './badge';

// Origin, path and query only; the #fragment is deliberately dropped.
const toPageKey = (href: string) => {
  const url = new URL(href, window.location.href);
  return `${url.origin}${url.pathname}${url.search}`;
};

export const CurrentPageBadge: React.FC<{
  href: string;
  currentHref: string;
}> = ({ href, currentHref }) => {
  let isCurrent = false;

  try {
    isCurrent = toPageKey(href) === toPageKey(currentHref);
  } catch {
    // Unparseable URLs can't match the current page.
  }

  if (!isCurrent) return null;

  return (
    <Badge
      variant='accent'
      label={
        <FormattedMessage
          id='current_page_badge.label'
          defaultMessage='You are here'
        />
      }
    />
  );
};
```

Choices I made:
- **Reuses the existing `Badge`** from `components/badge/index.tsx` rather than new markup or styles, so it picks up the redesign styling automatically. I used the `accent` variant; change it if you want a different look.
- **Label is translated** via `FormattedMessage` with the id `current_page_badge.label`. Mastodon's locale files are generated from message ids, so that key will need extracting before it appears in translations.
- **Relative `href`s** resolve against `window.location.href`. An unparseable URL renders nothing instead of throwing.

